{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>See, I have set before you this day life and good.</b> From here it follows that free will is given to man and the choice is in his hand to do good or evil, for were it not so, there would be no place for the Torah, and the punishment and the reward would be nullified. And the sense of 'the life and the good' is in the fulfillment of the Torah, and 'the death and the evil' if they do not fulfill it. Or one may say that 'the life and the good' and 'the death and the evil' refer to the Torah itself, in the manner of what is said (Ezekiel 20): ״וגם אני נתתי להם חקים לא טובים ומשפטים לא יחיו בהם״ — for the Torah is life and good to the righteous, and to the wicked it is death and evil." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret 'See, I have set before you this day life and good' as follows: that Scripture juxtaposed this pericope to the pericope above it, which speaks of the promise of redemption. And since the time of the resurrection of the dead is destined to occur close to the time of redemption — from the letters of ראה — therefore He juxtaposed to the pericope of redemption, 'See, I have set before you this day life and good.' And from this Solomon said (Song of Songs 8:12): ״האלף לך שלמה ומאתים לנוטרים את פריו״ — 'the thousand is yours, O Solomon, and two hundred for those who guard its fruit' — ומאתים, that is, six years over two hundred, and it is revealed to you that the time of the resurrection of the dead will be two hundred and six after the coming of Solomon, that is, the coming of the Messiah, who is of the seed of Solomon. And behold, the juxtaposition of the word ראה alludes to the time that is adjacent." "<b>And</b> the reason the pericope of redemption above was concluded with the word לעשותו ('to do it') was in order to liken the matter of redemption to the act of Creation, which was concluded (Genesis 2:3): ״אשר ברא אלהים לעשות״ — 'which Elohim created to do' — and He equated the end of time with the beginning of time, in order to inform you that they are equal regarding the matter of natural actions and the order of the world, for in the time of the Messiah the entire order of nature will proceed as in the act of Creation. And so they said explicitly: there is no difference between this world and the days of the Messiah except the subjection of foreign exile alone. And the addition of the letter vav alludes that the sixth millennium (האלף הששי) will follow the order of the six days of Creation. And immediately He juxtaposed to it, 'See, I have set before you this day life and good,' to inform that in it — in that time — the resurrection of the dead will occur. And from this He said, 'the life and the good,' for in that life they will merit the hidden good. And He wished to say that from the world of resurrection one will be transported to the World to Come, which is the hidden good; and this is what is meant by 'the life' — and that is the resurrection of the dead — and 'the good' — and that is the World to Come, which is after the resurrection, concerning which it is said (Psalms 31:20): ״מה רב טובך אשר צפנת ליראיך״. And 'the evil' — this is said of the sect of the wicked, whose resurrection will be for their evil, and they will be an abhorrence to all flesh, in the manner of what is written (Daniel 12:2): ״ורבים מישני אדמת עפר יקיצו אלה לחיי עולם ואלה לחרפות לדראון עולם״." "<b>And the view</b> of the Rambam, of blessed memory, regarding the resurrection of the dead is that those who arise in the resurrection will eat and drink and procreate, for the organs within them will not be for naught; and they will live long days, and after the resurrection they will die and come to the life of the World to Come. This is what the Sages, of blessed memory, always allude to when they say 'designated for the life of the World to Come' — that is the World to Come that is after the resurrection, concerning which the Sages, of blessed memory, said: the World to Come has no eating and no drinking, but only souls without bodies. For if there were bodies, the organs would be for naught, since they would not be used for bodily activities, which are eating and drinking and marital relations. And if you would say, this is no wonder, for behold we find a miraculous phenomenon in Moses and Elijah, who existed for a time in their bodies without eating and drinking — so too would the phenomenon be for the inhabitants of the World to Come, that they would persist in bodies like the persistence of Moses in his body at Mount Sinai, or like the persistence of Elijah. It is impossible to say so, for if the physical faculties were suspended in Moses and Elijah, that was only temporary, and there were no organs left for naught, since before the miracle they needed their organs and after the miracle they again needed them — their organs did not remain empty. But regarding the inhabitants of the World to Come, where there is no eating and no drinking — were the Holy One, blessed be He, to create a new world and they were to exist there in bodies with their organs idle — this is among the impossible, for a wise agent does not produce anything for naught. Therefore every thinking person must believe that the inhabitants of the World to Come have no bodies but souls alone. This is the general principle of the Rav's view, of blessed memory, and what emerges from his words." "<b>However,</b> this is not the view of the Sages of the Talmud and the masters of the Kabbalah, for they maintain that there is no death after the resurrection, as it is written (Isaiah 25): ״בלע המוט לנצח״, and from what the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted: the dead whom the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to revive will never again return to their dust. And concerning the world after the resurrection, the Sages, of blessed memory, said in Berakhot — and it was a constant saying in the mouth of Rav: the World to Come has no eating and no drinking and no jealousy and no competition, but the righteous sit with their crowns upon their heads and enjoy the radiance of the Shekhinah. And this saying surely demonstrates that there are bodies there, which is why they said 'it has no eating and no drinking' — for if there were no bodies there, there would be no need to say that souls have no eating and no drinking. Therefore we believe that the inhabitants of the World to Come will be in bodies, and the physical faculties will be suspended from them just as the physical faculties were suspended from Moses and Elijah, and their souls will be elevated above their bodies as was the case with Moses and Elijah. And if you would still press the difficulty and say that the organs would be for naught — they are not for naught, since they receive reward and pleasure in body and soul together, just as they labored in the fulfillment of the commandments and toiled in them as one, in body and soul; for the Holy One, blessed be He, does not withhold the reward of any creature, nor does He wish the body to be wronged in its judgment, but rather He wishes to reward it with pleasure that has no end, and that it be transported from the world of resurrection to the World to Come in body and soul. And so the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted: the Holy One, blessed be He, satisfies them from the radiance of the Shekhinah in the World to Come, as it is said (Psalms 17): ״אשבעה בהקיץ תמונתך״ — that is, that they will merit being sated from the radiance of the Shekhinah in their bodies, like the sustenance of the soul in this world through eating and drinking. And such is the view of the Ramban, of blessed memory, in Sha'ar ha-Gemul, and he brought this Midrash as proof — what they said in the Sifri regarding Moses our master, peace be upon him: 'He stored it away for the life of the World to Come,' that is, that he stored away his body for the life of the World to Come — not that it is so now. And so they said of Rabbi Akiva: 'Fortunate are you, Rabbi Akiva, for your soul departed with the word אחד — you are designated for the life of the World to Come.' <b>And in the chapter Ha-Ro'eh:</b> 'Blessed is He who put you to death in judgment and is destined to raise you to the life of the World to Come.' <b>And in Bereshit Rabbah:</b> ״ויברך אלהים את יום השביעי ויקדש אותו״ — 'the Holy One, blessed be He, blessed the World to Come, which begins with the seventh millennium'; ״ויקדש אותו״ — 'He designated it for Israel.' And this is the meaning of 'designated for the life of the World to Come.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:53.475874143Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}